<!-- Research Strategy / targeted enhancement: original four-card diagram retained -->
      <div class="nf-research-strategy-intro">
        <p class="description">01 / Research Strategy</p>
        <h3>Designing Research Around Three Questions</h3>
        <p>We designed a mixed-methods approach to connect Neurofit's conversion goals with the information needs, expectations, and decision-making processes of professional visitors.</p>
        <div class="nf-rq-grid" aria-label="Guiding research questions">
          <article class="nf-rq-card"><span>RQ 01 / Conversion Confidence</span><p>How might we help clinicians and disability management stakeholders feel informed and confident before prompting them to sign up?</p></article>
          <article class="nf-rq-card"><span>RQ 02 / Trust &amp; Perceived Value</span><p>What factors shape trust, understanding, and perceived value when professionals evaluate digital health platforms?</p></article>
          <article class="nf-rq-card"><span>RQ 03 / First-Impression Clarity</span><p>How might we help first-time visitors quickly understand what Neurofit offers, who it serves, and how it fits into their workflows?</p></article>
        </div>
        <p>I co-developed the research plan and recruitment strategy with another research lead, refining the approach with our faculty advisor and Neurofit's CEO. I independently designed the tree-testing instrument to examine navigation findability and label comprehension.</p>
        <p class="showcase-contribution"><strong>My Contribution</strong><br>Research Methodology Selection · Research Planning · Recruitment Strategy · Tree-Test Design · Stakeholder Collaboration</p>
      </div>

      <div class="nf-research-panel nf-synthesis-panel">
        <span>RESEARCH DESIGN RATIONALE</span>
        <h4>Complementary Evidence, One Research Strategy</h4>
        <p class="nf-diagram-intro">Secondary research established what the website communicated, how visitors engaged with it, and the market context. Primary research explored professional expectations and tested information organization. We compared these perspectives to develop research-informed design priorities.</p>
        <div class="nf-research-method-label"><strong>Secondary Research</strong><span>Existing website · Behavioural data · Market &amp; domain context</span></div>
        <!-- Original editable four-card diagram retained -->
        <div class="nf-synthesis-diagram" role="group" aria-label="Website audit, Google Analytics, competitor analysis, and literature review contribute to cross-method research synthesis">
          <div class="nf-diagram-card nf-diagram-audit"><strong>Website Audit</strong><p>Content, UX, accessibility</p></div>
          <div class="nf-diagram-card nf-diagram-analytics"><strong>Google Analytics</strong><p>Traffic and engagement</p></div>
          <div class="nf-diagram-card nf-diagram-competitor"><strong>Competitor Analysis</strong><p>Positioning and credibility</p></div>
          <div class="nf-diagram-card nf-diagram-literature"><strong>Literature Review</strong><p>Domain and content context</p></div>
          <svg class="nf-diagram-lines" viewBox="0 0 340 290" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M 85 66 L 170 145"/><path d="M 255 66 L 170 145"/><path d="M 85 230 L 170 145"/><path d="M 255 230 L 170 145"/></svg>
          <div class="nf-diagram-centre"><strong>Research<br>Synthesis</strong><span>Cross-method<br>interpretation</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="nf-research-bridge" aria-hidden="true"><span>+</span><span>Complementary perspectives</span></div>
        <div class="nf-diagram-context nf-primary-methods">
          <div class="nf-research-method-label"><strong>Primary Research</strong><span>Professional perspectives · Content organization · Findability</span></div>
          <div class="nf-primary-method-list"><span>Interviews</span><span>Closed Card Sorting</span><span>Tree Testing</span></div>
          <p>Primary research added context that behavioural data and website audits alone could not provide. Findings were compared across methods, not treated as independently validated.</p>
          
        </div>
        <div class="nf-cross-method-outcome"><strong>Cross-Method Synthesis</strong><p>Identify converging observations, unresolved questions, and research-informed design priorities.</p></div>
        <p class="nf-visual-caption">Primary research involved two company-connected domain experts. These exploratory findings are directional and do not represent the wider population of prospective users.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="nf-rq-evidence-map">
        <h4>Connecting Research Questions to Findings</h4>
        <p>Our synthesis linked each guiding question to relevant evidence and design opportunities. The relationships below are interpretive and can be refined as the case study is finalized.</p>
        <div class="nf-rq-map-list">
          <div class="nf-rq-map-item"><strong>RQ 01 / Conversion Confidence</strong><p>Interviews · Website Audit · Competitor Analysis <span>→</span> F01 Value Clarity · F04 Trust &amp; Credibility</p></div>
          <div class="nf-rq-map-item"><strong>RQ 02 / Trust &amp; Perceived Value</strong><p>Interviews · Competitor Analysis · Literature Review <span>→</span> F03 Audience Relevance · F04 Trust &amp; Credibility</p></div>
          <div class="nf-rq-map-item"><strong>RQ 03 / First-Impression Clarity</strong><p>Website Audit · Interviews · Card Sorting · Tree Testing <span>→</span> F01 Value Clarity · F02 Findability · F03 Audience Relevance</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="nf-visual-caption">GA4 informed understanding of engagement and conversion-measurement gaps; it did not directly measure visitor confidence or trust.</p>
      </div>

      
